{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 9169 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "88545c57506d8d9fc7f5a8d8c3d7a52884ac1491b4e81ce79ca703a00e27e5ae" :edb/db-before-t 9168 :edb/db-after-t 9169 :edb/tx-data [[13194139542481 50 #inst "2026-10-28T05:41:24.288Z" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186061861 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061866 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186064634 1071 "Calculate\n\n$$\n\\int x2^{x}dx\n$$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186064634 1071 "Calculate $\\intx2^{x}dx$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186064634 1074 "Use integration by parts with $u=x$ and $\\mathrm dv=2^x\\,\\mathrm dx$. Then $\\mathrm du=\\mathrm dx$ and $v=2^x/\\ln2$, so\n$$\n\\int x2^x\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{x2^x}{\\ln2}-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\int 2^x\\,\\mathrm dx\n=\\frac{2^x}{\\ln2}\\left(x-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\right)+C.\n$$\nDifferentiating the result gives $x2^x$, which verifies the antiderivative." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186065444 1064 "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\underbrace{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}_{u{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\underbrace{x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}_{uv}-\\int \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 1}_{v{u}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ and replace it with the simpler $$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ plus some additional terms." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186065444 1064 "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}{v}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & =\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}v}}-\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}1}}_{{\\color{red}v}{\\color{blue}{u}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ and replace it with the simpler $$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ plus some additional terms." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085088 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085093 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085468 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085469 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085473 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085474 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085478 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085480 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085483 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085485 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085489 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085493 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085494 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085496 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085499 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085501 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186085504 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085506 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186087269 1078 #edb/ref 17592186087273 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084926 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186088059 1074 "The equation is in standard form with $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=\\cos x$. An integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^x.\n$$\nMultiplying through by $e^x$ and using the product rule gives\n$$\ne^xy'+e^xy=e^x\\cos x,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(ye^x)=e^x\\cos x.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088140 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y=3,\\hspace{2em}x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088140 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{2}{x}y = 3, x > 0$.\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186088140 1074 "For $x>0$, an integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int(2/x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{2\\ln x}=x^2.\n$$\nMultiplying by $x^2$ and integrating gives\n$$\n(x^2y)'=3x^2,\\qquad x^2y=x^3+C,\\qquad y=x+\\frac{C}{x^2}.\n$$\nDifferentiation gives $y'+2y/x=3$, as required." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088141 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=1,\\hspace{2em}y(0)=-3\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088141 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 1, y(0) =-3\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186088141 1074 "An integrating factor is $I(x)=e^x$. Thus\n$$\n(e^xy)'=e^x,\\qquad e^xy=e^x+C,\\qquad y=1+Ce^{-x}.\n$$\nThe initial condition gives $-3=1+C$, so $C=-4$. Therefore\n$$\ny(x)=1-4e^{-x}=1-\\frac4{e^x}.\n$$\nThis satisfies both $y'+y=1$ and $y(0)=-3$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088142 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3x.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088142 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$2\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 3x$.\nSelect the correct options below.\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186088142 1074 "First divide by $2$:\n$$\ny'+\\frac12y=\\frac32x.\n$$\nAn integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{x/2}$. Integration by parts gives\n$$\n(ye^{x/2})'=\\frac32xe^{x/2},\\qquad ye^{x/2}=3(x-2)e^{x/2}+C.\n$$\nHence\n$$\ny=3(x-2)+Ce^{-x/2}.\n$$\nSubstitution verifies $2y'+y=3x$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088143 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088143 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - 2y = x^{2}e^{2x}$.\nSelect the correct options below.\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186088143 1074 "An integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{-2x}$. Multiplying and integrating gives\n$$\n(ye^{-2x})'=x^2,\\qquad ye^{-2x}=\\frac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\ny=\\frac{x^3e^{2x}}3+Ce^{2x}.\n$$\nDifferentiating verifies $y'-2y=x^2e^{2x}$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088436 1074 "Here $P(x)=2x$, so an integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int 2x\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{x^2}.\n$$\nMultiplication by $I(x)$ gives the exact derivative\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(ye^{x^2})=e^{x^2}\\cdot 2xe^{-x^2}.\n$$\nThe product rule verifies the left side is $e^{x^2}(y'+2xy)$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186088859 1064 "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186088859 1064 "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186098271 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098273 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186098289 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098291 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186100725 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186100725 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186100795 1074 "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186100795 1074 "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-2} & =A({\\color{red}-1})^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186100801 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186100801 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186101156 1074 "Substitute the initial condition into the given family:\n$$\n3=Ae^{2\\cdot1},\\qquad A=\\frac3{e^2}.\n$$\nFor this value, $y(1)=3$, and $y'=2Ae^{2x}=2y$ verifies the differential equation." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186101159 1074 "Substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$:\n$$\n4=\\frac1{A-1},\\qquad A-1=\\frac14,\\qquad A=\\frac54.\n$$\nThe resulting solution satisfies $y'=1/(5/4-x)^2=y^2$ on the interval $x<5/4$ containing the initial point $x=1$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186102031 1064 "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ \\underbrace{2{e}^{2x}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot \\underbrace{{e}^{2x}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186102031 1064 "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ \\underbrace{2{e}^{2x}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot \\underbrace{{e}^{2x}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186107243 1078 #edb/ref 17592186107247 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186109951 1078 #edb/ref 17592186109954 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186109957 1078 #edb/ref 17592186109961 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186110281 1078 #edb/ref 17592186110282 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186110892 1074 "Isolate the exponential term and take logarithms:\n$$\n10^{2x}=62,\\qquad 2x\\ln10=\\ln62,\\qquad x=\\frac{\\ln62}{2\\ln10}\\approx0.896196.\n$$\nRounded to three decimal places, $x=0.896$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186111371 1074 "Take logarithms of both positive sides:\n$$\n(2-3x)\\ln2=\\ln12,\\qquad x=\\frac{2-\\ln12/\\ln2}{3}\\approx-0.528321.\n$$\nRounded to three decimal places, $x=-0.528$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186111372 1074 "Isolate $3^y$:\n$$\n4\\cdot3^y=24,\\qquad3^y=6.\n$$\nSince $3^y$ is strictly increasing, the unique real solution is\n$$\ny=\\log_3 6=\\frac{\\ln6}{\\ln3}.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186111426 1074 "Take natural logarithms:\n$$\ny\\ln3=\\ln23,\\qquad y=\\frac{\\ln23}{\\ln3}\\approx2.854050.\n$$\nRounded to three decimal places, $y=2.854$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186111879 1064 "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186111879 1064 "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{{\\color{blue}2}}({{\\color{blue}2}}^{x}) & ={log}_{{\\color{blue}2}}(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186113833 1078 #edb/ref 17592186113836 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186114205 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114207 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186114211 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114214 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186114217 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114219 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186114349 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114350 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186115816 1074 "Exponential functions are continuous, and the denominator is nonzero at $x=0$. Direct substitution gives\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{4^x-5}{2^x-2}=\\frac{1-5}{1-2}=4.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186115878 1074 "By continuity of the exponential function,\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to3}10^{3x-7}=10^{3\\cdot3-7}=10^2=100.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186115879 1074 "As $x\\to\\infty$, the exponent $2x+4$ tends to $\\infty$. Since the base $2>1$,\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}2^{2x+4}=\\infty.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186115880 1074 "Write $8^{-x}=1/8^x$. Since $8^x\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$,\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}8^{-x}=0.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186115902 1074 "Because $6>1$, $6^x\\to0$ as $x\\to-\\infty$. Multiplication by the constant $-7$ preserves this limit:\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}(-7\\cdot6^x)=0.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186121313 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121314 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186121319 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121324 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186121439 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121443 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186121457 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121458 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186124786 1074 "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate term by term:\n$$\nf_y=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy^2)+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2\\cos y)=2xy-x^2\\sin y.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186124787 1074 "Treat $y$ as constant:\n$$\nf_x=y^2\\sec^2x-\\frac{y}{x^2}.\n$$\nSince $\\sec^2\\pi=1$,\n$$\nf_x(\\pi,2)=4-\\frac2{\\pi^2}.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186124807 1074 "The partial derivative with respect to $y$ keeps $x$ fixed and increments only $y$. Its defining difference quotient is\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+h}-x\\sqrt y}{h}.\n$$\nFor the usual two-sided derivative, this limit is taken at $y>0$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186124810 1074 "Treat $y$ as constant:\n$$\nf_x=2^x\\ln2\\tan y-\\frac{2y}{\\pi x^3}.\n$$\nAt $(1,\\pi/4)$, $\\tan(\\pi/4)=1$, so\n$$\nf_x(1,\\pi/4)=2\\ln2-\\frac{2(\\pi/4)}\\pi=2\\ln2-\\frac12.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186125867 1064 "Consider the multivariable function $f(x,y),$ given by\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ we \"fix\" the variable $y$ (in other words, treat $y$ as if it were a constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (1)+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the term $e^{y}$ vanished when taking the derivative with respect to $x.$ This is because $e^y$ is a function of $y$ only, and since we're treating $y$ as a constant, this entire term is treated as a constant and therefore differentiates to zero (just as a constant would).\n\nSimilarly, to compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ we fix the variable $x$ and then take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2y)+({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy+{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we wish to evaluate a partial derivative at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ we can use any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f(x_0,y_0)} {\\partial x} =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\\Bigg|_{(x_0,y_0)} = f_x(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nThe notations for $f_y(x_0,y_0)$ follow the same patterns." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186125867 1064 "Consider the multivariable function $f(x,y),$ given by\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ we \"fix\" the variable $y$ (in other words, treat $y$ as if it were a constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{\\color{blue}x}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({\\color{blue}x})+\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (1)+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the term $e^{y}$ vanished when taking the derivative with respect to $x.$ This is because $e^y$ is a function of $y$ only, and since we're treating $y$ as a constant, this entire term is treated as a constant and therefore differentiates to zero (just as a constant would).\n\nSimilarly, to compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ we fix the variable $x$ and then take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{\\color{blue}y}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}(x{{\\color{blue}y}}^{2}+{e}^{{\\color{blue}y}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}({{\\color{blue}y}}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}({e}^{{\\color{blue}y}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2y)+({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy+{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we wish to evaluate a partial derivative at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ we can use any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f(x_0,y_0)} {\\partial x} =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\\Bigg|_{(x_0,y_0)} = f_x(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nThe notations for $f_y(x_0,y_0)$ follow the same patterns." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186144137 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144142 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186144143 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144147 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186147391 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\