l-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.22$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186168010 1064 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\sin   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{t/2} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 4 t ) \\\\ \\cos   ( 4 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},\n$$\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.22$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186174934 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174938 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186174940 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174941 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186174946 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174951 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186174952 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174953 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186174958 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174961 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186174970 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174973 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186176466 1074 "The model has solution $P(t)=P_0e^{rt}$. Doubling after ten years gives $e^{10r}=2$, so $r=\\ln2/10$. For tripling,\n$$\ne^{rt}=3,\\qquad t=\\frac{\\ln3}{r}=\\frac{10\\ln3}{\\ln2}\\approx15.8496.\n$$\nIt took **15.8 years**, to one decimal place." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186176467 1074 "The model has solution $T(t)=T_0e^{-rt}$. Reducing **by** four-fifths leaves one-fifth, so $e^{-2r}=1/5$ and $r=\\ln5/2$. Reducing to one-fifteenth requires\n$$\ne^{-rt}=\\frac1{15},\\qquad t=\\frac{\\ln15}{r}=\\frac{2\\ln15}{\\ln5}\\approx3.3652.\n$$\nThe required time is **3.4 minutes**, to one decimal place." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186176468 1074 "For $P'=-0.1P$, populations four years apart satisfy\n$$\nP(9)=P(5)e^{-0.1(9-5)}=3200e^{-0.4}\\approx2145.024.\n$$\nThus the model predicts approximately **2145 penguins**, to the nearest whole penguin." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186176469 1074 "The model is $P(t)=P_0e^{0.09t}$. At $t=18$,\n$$\n8000=P_0e^{1.62},\\qquad P_0=8000e^{-1.62}\\approx1583.190.\n$$\nThe initial population was approximately **1583 fish**, to the nearest whole fish." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186176470 1074 "With $P(0)=150000$, the solution is $P(t)=150000e^{0.055t}$. Set $P(t)=400000$ and solve:\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\ln(400000/150000)}{0.055}\\approx17.8333.\n$$\nRounded to the nearest year, the answer is **18 years**." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186176472 1074 "With $N(0)=150000$, the solution is $N(t)=150000e^{-0.05t}$. Reaching $50000$ particles requires\n$$\ne^{-0.05t}=\\frac13,\\qquad t=\\frac{\\ln3}{0.05}\\approx21.9722.\n$$\nRounded to the nearest year, this is **22 years**." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186189634 1078 #edb/ref 17592186189639 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186190978 1078 #edb/ref 17592186190980 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186190984 1078 #edb/ref 17592186190986 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186191026 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191031 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186192183 1078 #edb/ref 17592186192186 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186192958 1074 "Treat $x$ and $y$ as constants and apply the chain rule:\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=y\\cdot\\frac12(x^2+z^2)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2z=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{x^2+z^2}}.\n$$\nThe formula holds when $x^2+z^2>0$. At $x=z=0$, this partial derivative exists only when $y=0$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186193182 1074 "Treat $x$ as constant. The product rule gives\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=0+x\\left(\\sin y+y\\cos y\\right)=x(\\sin y+y\\cos y).\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186193183 1074 "Using the quotient rule while holding $y$ fixed,\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=\\frac{-(x+y)\\sin x-\\cos x}{(x+y)^2}.\n$$\nAt $(\\pi,0)$, the denominator is nonzero and\n$$\nf_x(\\pi,0)=\\frac{-\\pi\\sin\\pi-\\cos\\pi}{\\pi^2}=\\frac1{\\pi^2}.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186193190 1074 "The quotient rule gives\n$$\nf_y=\\frac{(2ye^{y^2})(x^2-2y)-e^{y^2}(-2)}{(x^2-2y)^2}\n=\\frac{(2x^2y-4y^2+2)e^{y^2}}{(x^2-2y)^2}.\n$$\nThus $h(x,y)=2x^2y-4y^2+2$. The derivative is defined where $x^2-2y\\ne0$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186193376 1074 "Treat $x$ as constant and apply the chain rule:\n$$\nf_y=e^{4x-x^4y}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(4x-x^4y)=-x^4e^{4x-x^4y}.\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186193890 1064 "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot {\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot {\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})+3{\\color{blue}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot {\\color{blue}x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot {\\color{blue}x}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186193890 1064 "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186216441 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216443 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216446 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216448 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216448 1080 "\\exp (\\int g(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216448 1080 "\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186216449 1080 "\\exp (-\\int g(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216449 1080 "\\exp (-\\intg(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186216622 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216625 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216627 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216629 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216632 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216636 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216637 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216639 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216639 1080 "\\exp (\\int g(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216639 1080 "\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186216640 1080 "\\exp (-\\int g(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216640 1080 "\\exp (-\\intg(x)dx)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186216641 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216642 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216646 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216648 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216651 1078 #edb/ref 17592186216653 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216653 1080 "\\exp (-\\int h(y)dy)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216653 1080 "\\exp (-\\inth(y)dy)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186216654 1080 "\\exp (\\int h(y)dy)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186216654 1080 "\\exp (\\inth(y)dy)" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217762 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=2xy-x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217762 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} = 2xy - x$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})\n$$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217762 1074 "For $P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy=0$, an integrating factor $I(x)$ must satisfy\n$$\nI P_y=I'Q+I Q_x,\\qquad \\frac{I'}I=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}=g(x).\n$$\nConsequently\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\mathrm dx\\right).\n$$\nFor this equation take $P=x-2xy$, $Q=1$. Then $g(x)=-2x$ and $I(x)=e^{-x^2}$; direct differentiation verifies exactness. The ratio criterion is used where its denominator is nonzero." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216597 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216602 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217793 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-({x}^{3}+2{y}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217793 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$x^{2}ydx - (x^{3} + 2y^{4})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) = \\frac{4}{y}\n$$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(x, y) = x^{2}y, Q(x, y) =-x^{3} - 2y^{4}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(y) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217795 1071 "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n(y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)={e}^{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that the function $I(y)={e}^{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217795 1071 "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$(y + x)dx + xdy = 0$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y) = e^{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that the function $I(y) = e^{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$(xe^{y})dx + ((y + x)e^{y})dy$ {{field-1}}" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217795 1074 "Multiply each coefficient of the original equation by $I(y)=e^y$. The equation to test is\n$$\n((y+x)e^y)\\,\\mathrm dx+(xe^y)\\,\\mathrm dy=0.\n$$\nThis is the required transformed equation. In fact,\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}((y+x)e^y)=(1+x+y)e^y,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(xe^y)=e^y.\n$$\nThese are not identically equal, so $e^y$ does **not** make this equation exact. Selecting the product equation alone does not establish that it is an integrating factor." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217796 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n(7x-4xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy-7x),\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217796 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$(7x - 4xy)dx + dy = 0$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})\n$$ {{field-1}} where $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$.Let's denote\n$P(x, y) = - (4xy - 7x), Q(x, y) = 1$.Now, we compute the partial derivatives:$-4x$ {{field-3}} $1$ {{field-4}}" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217796 1074 "For an integrating factor depending only on $x$, exactness requires\n$$\n\\frac{I'}I=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}=g(x),\\qquad I(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\mathrm dx\\right).\n$$\nHere $P=7x-4xy$ and $Q=1$, so\n$$\nP_y=-4x,\\qquad Q_x=0.\n$$\nThus $g(x)=-4x$ and $I(x)=e^{-2x^2}$. These identify the four requested selections in their existing fields." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217797 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=-x({y}^{2}+1)\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186217797 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} =-x(y^{2} + 1)$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})\n$$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\inth(y)dy)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{y}$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186217797 1074 "For an integrating factor depending only on $y$, exactness requires\n$$\nI P_y+I'P=I Q_x,\\qquad\\frac{I'}I=-\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P}=-h(y).\n$$\nTherefore the requested selections are\n$$\nh(y)=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P},\\qquad I(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm dy\\right).\n$$\nHere $P=x(y^2+1)$, $Q=1$, giving $h(y)=2y/(1+y^2)$ where the ratio is defined. Hence $I(y)=1/(1+y^2)$; this factor extends to every real $y$ and makes the equation exact even at $x=0$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725842 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228039 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725842 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228041 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725840 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228041 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725840 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228059 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725843 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228059 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725843 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228080 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725839 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228080 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725839 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228109 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725841 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186228109 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725841 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186229626 1074 "Try $y_p=Be^{2x}$, so $y_p'=2Be^{2x}$. Substitution gives\n$$\n9(2Be^{2x})+18Be^{2x}=4e^{2x},\\qquad36B=4.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\frac19e^{2x}.\n$$\nDirect substitution verifies the equation; the complementary term is not included in this particular solution." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186229628 1074 "Try $y_p=Be^{2x}$. Substitution into $y'-6y=8e^{2x}$ gives $(2-6)B=8$, so $B=-2$. Adding the complementary solution,\n$$\ny=Ae^{6x}-2e^{2x}.\n$$\nThe initial condition gives $1=A-2$, so $A=3$ and\n$$\ny(x)=3e^{6x}-2e^{2x}.\n$$\nIts residual $y'-6y-8e^{2x}$ is zero and $y(0)=1$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186229635 1074 "The forcing term has the same exponential form as the complementary solution, so try $y_p=Bxe^{4x}$. Then\n$$\ny_p'=Be^{4x}+4Bxe^{4x},\\qquad y_p'-4y_p=Be^{4x}.\n$$\nThus $B=3$. Adding the complementary solution gives\n$$\ny(x)=(A+3x)e^{4x},\\qquad A\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\nThis family satisfies $y'-4y=3e^{4x}$." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186229641 1074 "Try $y_p=Be^{-3x}$, so $y_p'=-3Be^{-3x}$. Substituting gives\n$$\n5(-3Be^{-3x})+10Be^{-3x}=15e^{-3x},\\qquad-5B=15.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\ny_p(x)=-3e^{-3x}.\n$$\nDirect substitution verifies the equation." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186229649 1074 "Because $e^{3x}$ already occurs in the complementary solution, try $y_p=Bxe^{3x}$. Substitution gives\n$$\ny_p'-3y_p=Be^{3x}=9e^{3x},\n$$\nso $B=9$. The general solution is $y=(A+9x)e^{3x}$. The condition $y(0)=2$ gives $A=2$, hence\n$$\ny(x)=(2+9x)e^{3x}.\n$$\nBoth the differential equation and initial condition are satisfied." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186235523 1071 "Find the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186235523 1071 "Find the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186235523 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186235523 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186244195 1074 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡y$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ only." 13194139542481 17592186576411 true] [17592186244195 1074 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y}\\right)=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ only." 13194139542481 17592186576411 false] [17592186244216 1071 "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential eq